16:["$","header",null,{"className":"article-header","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"breadcrumbs","aria-label":"Breadcrumb","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/en/","children":"THE ORGANIC CLUB"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"/"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/en/topics/creative/","children":"Creative culture"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"/"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"proof object"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mono acid","style":{"marginTop":28},"children":["FIELD GUIDE"," ","· ","104"]}],["$","h1",null,{"children":"After the problem hook, should an app demo show the result glimpse early? Usually, yes."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"article-dek","children":"This is a narrower question than problem-first versus screen-first: once you have named the viewer’s job, an early glimpse of the finished output usually makes a search-intent demo easier to evaluate."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"byline","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/en/about/#organic-club-editorial","className":"author-signature","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/images/authors/editorial-desk.jpg","alt":"","width":48,"height":48}],["$","span",null,{"children":["The Editorial Desk",["$","small",null,{"children":"The club’s editorial desk"}]]}]]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"muted","children":["17/09/2026"," ","· ",11," min"]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"evidence-tag","children":"Editorial analysis"}]]}]]}]
17:["$","figure",null,{"style":{"margin":0},"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"article-hero article-hero-with-case","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/images/daily/result-glimpse-after-problem-app-demo.jpg","alt":"Editorial illustration of an app demo setup with a user problem note in front and a finished digital output visible on a nearby screen.","width":1536,"height":1024,"fetchPriority":"high"}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"image-caption","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"https://theorganiclub.com/images/daily/result-glimpse-after-problem-app-demo.jpg","children":"THE ORGANIC CLUB Studio"}]," · ",["$","a",null,{"href":"https://theorganiclub.com/en/about/#images","children":"Original artwork"}]," · ","Original AI illustration."]}]]}]
18:["$","div",null,{"className":"article-layout","children":[["$","aside",null,{"className":"article-aside","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mono acid","children":"IN THIS STORY"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"#definition","children":["01 / ","The essential"]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"#case-notes","children":"02 / Watch & break down"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"#analysis","children":"03 / The argument"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"#deep-dive","children":"04 / Apply the idea"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"#tokportal-workflow","children":"↳ Inside TokPortal"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"#practice","children":["05 / ","In practice"]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"#sources","children":["06 / ","Sources & limits"]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/en/read/creative/result-glimpse-after-problem-app-demo/article.md","children":"↗ Plain-text edition"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/en/lexicon/","children":["↗ ","The AGD lexicon"]}]]}],["$","article",null,{"className":"article-text","children":[["$","section",null,{"className":"quick-answer","id":"definition","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mono acid","children":"THE TAKEAWAY"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"For TikTok search-intent app demos, a practical default is problem → result glimpse → minimum steps → inspectable return."}]]}],["$","section",null,{"id":"case-notes","className":"case-notes","children":[["$","$L1b","studyfetch-mnemonic",{"example":"$1:props:children:1:props:article:examples:0","lens":"The StudyFetch archival case is the better fit because it is already an app-demo example. Its visible sequence supports a narrow structural lesson: a specific study problem leads into a corresponding product prompt and then a visible answer. Readers should also see its limitations and archival date clearly: checked 2026-09-09, muted visual inspection, no claim about endorsement, accuracy, distribution, or conversion."}]]}],["$","$L1c",null,{"a":"$1:props:children:1:props:article","lang":"en"}],"$undefined",["$","section",null,{"id":"analysis","className":"article-prose","children":[["$","$1d","0",{"children":[["$","$1d","0",{"children":[["$","p","0",{"children":["This article addresses a narrower editing decision than our separate piece on whether an app demo should begin with the problem or with the product screen. Here, assume the opening problem is already on screen. The next question is what to do immediately after that: cut into the workflow, or briefly show the finished output first. For search-intent demos, I think the stronger default is usually a short result glimpse. That framing is intentionally distinct from the opening-hook question: the earlier article is about the first orientation move; this one is about the proof move that follows it."]}]]}]]}],["$","$1d","1",{"children":[["$","$1d","0",{"children":[["$","p","0",{"children":["TikTok’s documentation provides context, not a creative rulebook. TikTok says Search helps people explore and learn, and says Creator Search Insights can surface often-searched and content-gap topics in select regions [1]. From that, the cautious inference is that query-fit matters. It does not follow that one edit sequence is officially preferred, or that a result glimpse will outperform every other structure. My claim is editorial and conditional: when someone arrives with a narrow practical query, a fast look at the solved state can help them decide sooner whether the video matches the job they came to solve."]}]]}]]}],["$","$1d","2",{"children":[["$","$1d","0",{"children":[["$","p","0",{"children":["The recurring app-demo failure is not “too little product.” It is procedure without destination. Teams say “show the feature,” then the video becomes taps, menus, and cursor movement before the audience knows what those actions are building toward. A result glimpse changes the meaning of the steps that follow. The viewer is no longer waiting to discover the point of the demo; they are watching how a visible output was produced. That difference matters most when the searcher is trying to answer a concrete question such as: Will this make a usable quiz card, a clean summary, a categorized expense sheet, or a caption draft I can actually inspect?"]}]]}]]}],"$L1e","$L1f","$L20","$L21","$L22","$L23"]}],"$L24",false,"$L25","$L26","$undefined","$L27","$L28","$L29","$L2a"]}],"$L2b"]}]
19:["$","section",null,{"className":"related-grid","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"section-heading","children":["$","h2",null,{"children":"Other currents."}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"article-grid","children":[["$","$L2c","102",{"a":{"id":"102","author":"organic-club-editorial","slug":"problem-first-or-screen-first-app-demo","category":"creative","term":"Problem-first opening","kind":"guide","image":"problem-first-or-screen-first-app-demo","media":{"kind":"illustration","src":"/images/daily/problem-first-or-screen-first-app-demo.jpg","width":1536,"height":1024,"alt":{"en":"A photographic-style desk scene with a phone filming, handwritten study notes in front, and a softly blurred laptop behind, suggesting the choice between leading with the problem or the app screen."},"credit":"THE ORGANIC CLUB Studio","provenance":"Original AI-generated editorial illustration. Illustrative scene, not documentary evidence.","source":"https://theorganiclub.com/images/daily/problem-first-or-screen-first-app-demo.jpg","license":"Original artwork","licenseUrl":"https://theorganiclub.com/en/about/#images"},"en":{"title":"Should your app demo start with the problem or the screen? Usually, name the problem first.","dek":"If the feature and promise stay fixed, leading with the user’s situation is often the more interpretable AGD test for app discovery content."},"readingMinutes":12},"lang":"en"}],["$","$L2c","010",{"a":{"id":"010","author":"spike","slug":"attention-war","category":"doctrine","term":"Attention War","kind":"essay","image":"attention-war","media":{"kind":"photograph","src":"/images/editorial/attention-war.jpg","width":1280,"height":720,"alt":{"en":"Crowds move below towering illuminated billboards in Times Square at night."},"credit":"ScottandYanling","provenance":"Wikimedia Commons photograph. Resized and JPEG-compressed from the source photograph. Original source SHA1: 5ddae25d04c3d3d4ad78cd0745a1f27946fe5fa5.","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Times_Square_at_Night_with_crowds_of_people.jpg","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0"},"en":{"title":"Organic Social Media for Apps: A StudyFetch TikTok Breakdown","dek":"A creator’s StudyFetch video moves from one awkward study problem to the actual app. Here is the structure an AGD team can use in its own product demos."},"readingMinutes":7},"lang":"en"}],["$","$L2c","029",{"a":{"id":"029","author":"valentine","slug":"creative-brief","category":"creative","term":"Creative Brief","kind":"guide","image":"creative-brief","media":{"kind":"photograph","src":"/images/editorial/creative-brief.jpg","width":1280,"height":924,"alt":{"en":"An archival photograph of printers working at benches in the Deli Courant typesetting workshop."},"credit":"Rijksmuseum","provenance":"Wikimedia Commons photograph. Resized and JPEG-compressed from the source photograph. Original source SHA1: bdc598ccc83875350e8488c22995634e31cdafff.","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zetterij_van_de_Deli_Courant,_NG-1990-14-39.jpg","license":"CC0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en"},"en":{"title":"How to Write a TikTok Creative Brief with a Concrete Example","dek":"An AGD creative brief connects audience, promise, format and hypothesis."},"readingMinutes":4},"lang":"en"}]]}]]}]
